### Solution for Angular Displacement

Given values:
• Length \(L = 1\) m
• Radius \(r = 4\) cm \(= 4 \times 10^{-2}\) m
• Shear force \(F = 10^5\) N
• Shear modulus \(G = 10^{10}\) N/m\(^2\)
The shear modulus \(G\) is defined as the ratio of shear stress (\(\sigma_{\text{shear}}\)) to shear strain (\(\theta\)).
G = \frac{\sigma_{\text{shear}}}{\theta}
Shear stress is given by \(\sigma_{\text{shear}} = \frac{F}{A}\), where \(A\) is the cross-sectional area of the rod. For a cylindrical rod, \(A = \pi r^2\).
Substituting the expression for shear stress into the shear modulus formula:
G = \frac{F/(A)}{\theta} = \frac{F}{A\theta}
Rearranging to solve for the angular displacement \(\theta\):
\theta = \frac{F}{GA}
Now, substitute the given values:
\theta = \frac{10^5}{10^{10} \times \pi \times (4 \times 10^{-2})^2}
\theta = \frac{10^5}{10^{10} \times \pi \times 16 \times 10^{-4}}
\theta = \frac{10^5}{16\pi \times 10^{10-4}}
\theta = \frac{10^5}{16\pi \times 10^6}
\theta = \frac{1}{16\pi \times 10^{6-5}}
\theta = \frac{1}{16\pi \times 10}
\theta = \frac{1}{160\pi}\text{ Radian}
The angular displacement \(\theta\) is \(\frac{1}{160\pi}\) Radian.

### Step 1: Calculate image position for the convex mirror

For a convex mirror, the object distance \(u = -30\text{ cm}\) and focal length \(f = +30\text{ cm}\). Using the mirror formula:
\frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}
\frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{-30} = \frac{1}{30}
\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{30} + \frac{1}{30}
\frac{1}{v} = \frac{2}{30} = \frac{1}{15}
v = 15\text{ cm}
The image formed by the convex mirror is at \(15\text{ cm}\) behind the convex mirror. The distance of this image from the object is \(30\text{ cm} + 15\text{ cm} = 45\text{ cm}\).

### Step 2: Determine the position of the plane mirror

For the images produced by both mirrors to coincide, the plane mirror must be placed midway between the object and the image formed by the convex mirror. This means the plane mirror should be placed at a distance of \(\frac{45}{2} = 22.5\text{ cm}\) from the object.

### Step 3: Calculate the distance between the two mirrors

The object is \(30\text{ cm}\) from the convex mirror. The plane mirror is placed \(22.5\text{ cm}\) from the object. Therefore, the distance between the two mirrors is:
\text{Distance} = 30\text{ cm} - 22.5\text{ cm} = 7.5\text{ cm}
The distance between the two mirrors is 7.5 cm.

